From 27806448880b5be80171fa35c431fb9e70b4897d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Jeremiah J. Gassensmith" <jgassens@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 16:18:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Audit substituent subgraph coverage so unnameable branches
 decline

A fragment the namer cannot express is currently dropped from the generated name
while its atoms are still counted as named, producing a confident,
OPSIN-parseable name for a smaller molecule:

    NC(=O)CNc1ccc([As](=O)(O)O)cc1  ->  "2-(phenylamino)acetamide"

The whole -As(=O)(O)O group is gone, and no error is reported. The same arsonic
acid as a PARENT already declines correctly with

    UnnamedAtomError: Generated name '<component>' left unnamed atoms: 6:As, ...

so the engine already knows it cannot name it. The check simply never runs at the
substituent level: assert_component_fully_named is called once, in
component_namer, and name_subgraph never calls it. namer.py then records
SubstituentItem(atom_ids=branch_atoms), claiming every atom in the branch
regardless of what the branch name covered, so the component-level audit is
satisfied by an over-claiming binding.

This applies the existing rule one level deeper. The shortcut paths
(heteroatom-subgraph renderer, direct functional prefix) need no check because
each matches the complete component by construction.

Coverage is computed from the atom_ids the parts already carry, mirroring
name_assembly._consumed_bond_ids, rather than through refresh_name_atom_bindings.
That matters: the audit runs on every branch of every molecule, and rebuilding
bindings measured ~45% slower over 5k structures (101 -> 56 structures/s), while
reading the existing sets costs nothing measurable (94.8 -> 95.3/s).

Measured on 4,999 NCI structures (rdkit Data/NCI/first_5K.smi), scoring by
round-tripping each generated name back through OPSIN 2.9.0:

                     before   after
    round-trip         4724    4724     <- unchanged, no correct name lost
    declined            204     210
    misnamed             36      30
    name-unparseable     27      27
    error                 8       8

All six that moved are arsenic-as-substituent cases; nothing else changed. The
remaining 30 misnamings are separate causes (mostly same-formula connectivity
differences) and are untouched here.

The same silent-omission shape also affects isotope labels ([13CH3]C(=O)O ->
"acetic acid") and radicals (TEMPO -> "1-hydroxy-2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine").
Those are not fixed by this change, since they are dropped elsewhere, but they
are the same class of failure and worth tracking.

Tests: five related arsenic substituent cases from the same family, unit tests
for the new audit in both directions, and a guard that ordinary names are
unaffected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
